/**
 * SDK-owned one-shot QR-expiry watch (STORY-60), shared by both SDKs and both platforms.
 *
 * When a payment QR is created the SDK facade calls [watch] with the QR's own expiry; the scheduler
 * fires [onExpired] **exactly once**, when the expiry passes, so the app can blank the code — an
 * expired QR must never stay scannable (ISSUE-73), and pushing the timer into the SDK makes that
 * the default every integrator gets rather than per-app timer logic.
 *
 * Lifecycle: at most one watch is active. [watch] **supersedes** any prior watch (only one QR is on
 * screen at a time); [cancel] stops the active watch — call it on teardown so the callback can't
 * fire into dead UI. An expiry already in the past fires [onExpired] on the next loop turn.
 *
 * The injected [scope] decides which thread [onExpired] runs on: the facades pass a
 * `Dispatchers.Main` scope so the callback lands on the UI thread. Kept dispatcher-agnostic here so
 * it is unit-testable on a virtual-time test scheduler.
 */
