/**
 * Tells the app when a sale's funds have been confirmed in the merchant's bank account (STORY-95).
 *
 * When a payment is approved **and** the backend said the beneficiary bank supports credit
 * confirmation, the **SDK owns the polling** — exponential backoff for up to 30 days — and uses
 * this channel to notify the app; the app reacts to the callback, it does not poll the gateway
 * itself. The same deliberate choices as [TransactionResolvedObserver], for the same reasons:
 *
 *  * **Registered once, at SDK level — not per payment.** Funds can land minutes or days after the
 *    sale, long after the process that took the payment is gone; registered here, it fires for any
 *    transaction whose confirmation concludes, including one from an earlier process.
 *  * **It carries the reference.** The poller walks every eligible row, so this can fire for
 *    different payments; the app matches its sale by [CreditConfirmation.reference].
 *  * **One fire site.** [MerchantTransactionStore.updateCreditConfirmation] is the single point
 *    where a row's confirmation concludes, so firing there covers every write path from one place
 *    (`devices/CLAUDE.md` §14).
 *  * **Only terminal answers.** `RECEIVED`, or the final 30-day `UNABLE_TO_CONFIRM` give-up. An
 *    in-flight failed attempt is never announced — a poll's failure is not an answer (§15.4).
 *  * **No replay on registration.** An app that was dead when the confirmation landed reads
 *    `creditConfirmationStatus` off the stored transaction instead — the read path stays.
 *
 * Additive and optional (`devices/CLAUDE.md` §6): an integration that never registers behaves
 * exactly as before. Lives in `commonMain` so Android, iOS and the React Native bridge share one
 * definition (§12 parity — note only Android runs the background poller; on iOS the app fetches
 * the confirmation manually, so this observer never fires there today).
 *
 * **Delivered on the main thread**, matching the payment callback — via the shared
 * [co.veyra.common.observer.ObserverDelivery] hop on Android and the iOS facade's
 * `Dispatchers.Main` scope on iOS (ISSUE-182).
 */
