/**
 * Tells the app when the merchant's backend status changes (STORY-110 concern 3).
 *
 * A merchant deactivated or suspended mid-session should stop the app taking payments **at once** —
 * not at the next screen load. Without this the SDK polls, writes its store and says nothing, so an
 * app that read `merchant.status` when its payment screen appeared keeps offering to take money
 * until something happens to make it read again.
 *
 * ## What was actually missing here, because it was not the observer
 *
 * On Android this is a notification gap: `MerchantStatusScheduler` polls (foreground-only) and
 * writes `MerchantDataStore`, so the fact existed and nobody was told. **On iOS the fact did not
 * exist**: there was no poller at all, only an on-demand `merchantStatus(merchantId)` fetch a host
 * could call, and a stored status that changed solely as a side effect of the host asking. So
 * "notify on iOS too" required building the thing to notify *about* first — an app-scoped iOS poll
 * driver (§16) — which is most of this concern's cost and is exactly §12's warning that the thin
 * platform is the expensive one. It is a real gap and not a §12 asymmetry: iOS has the rail (it
 * already fetches merchant status), it simply never watched it.
 *
 * ## Shape
 *
 * Single-listener, last registration wins, no replay — as every other STORY-110 channel. Delivered
 * on the main thread via the shared [co.veyra.common.observer.ObserverDelivery] hop (ISSUE-182).
 *
 * **The diff is done here, from values the platform supplies**, rather than each platform deciding
 * what counts as a change: both stores hold the previous status, so the comparison is against
 * persisted state and survives a restart — a merchant deactivated while the app was dead is
 * announced on the first poll after launch, which is the case an in-memory baseline would miss.
 */
