/**
 * Tells the app when the issuer has changed a card's status (STORY-110 concern 1).
 *
 * The problem this solves, which is the sharpest one in the story: an issuer suspends a card
 * server-side; the SDK's status poll applies it to the registry — and the customer is looking at a
 * card screen that still shows the card as usable. They tap. It fails at the terminal. The SDK
 * knew, possibly minutes earlier, and had no way to say so.
 *
 * The design choices mirror the merchant side's [co.veyra.softpos.payment.sdk.merchant.TransactionResolvedObserver]
 * exactly, and for the same reasons — this is deliberately not a new pattern:
 *
 *  * **Registered once, at SDK level — not per card.** The status of a card the app is *not*
 *    currently showing is precisely the case that bites, so a per-card registration would miss it.
 *  * **It carries the TUR.** The sweep walks every stored token, so this fires for different cards.
 *  * **One fire site.** [TokenRegistry.updateStatus] is the single point where a stored token's
 *    status changes — the Android `TokenStatusPollWorker` and the iOS scene-active sweep both
 *    reach it through the shared [TokenLifecycleSync], and the activation flows write through it
 *    too — so firing there covers every rail on both platforms from one place (§14) rather than a
 *    hook per caller.
 *  * **Only on a genuine transition.** A poll that re-applies the status a token already had fires
 *    nothing; an app is woken when something changed, not on every sweep. Comparison is on the raw
 *    stored value, case-insensitively, so `ACTIVE` → `active` is correctly *not* a change.
 *  * **No replay on registration.** An app that was dead when the status changed reads
 *    `getCards()` at startup — which is why the read path stays. This is a convenience over the
 *    store, not a guaranteed-delivery queue (§16: the store is the source of truth; the callback is
 *    only a notification, and an app that misses one loses nothing).
 *
 * Additive and optional (§6): an integration that never registers behaves exactly as before.
 * Lives in `commonMain` so Android, iOS and the React Native bridge share one API and one
 * definition of "the status changed" (§14).
 *
 * **Delivered on the main thread on every surface**, hopped in Kotlin: the fire site runs on
 * whatever thread the sweep is on, and delivery goes through the shared
 * [co.veyra.common.observer.ObserverDelivery] hop that each SDK installs at init on Android, and
 * through the iOS facade's `Dispatchers.Main` scope on iOS. (ISSUE-182 — this used to be promised
 * per wrapper and native Android had no wrapper, so it silently did not happen.)
 */
