/**
 * Tells the app when a wallet payment it was left waiting on has resolved (STORY-110 concern 4).
 *
 * **The gap this closes is an asymmetry, not an omission.** The merchant side has had
 * `TransactionResolvedObserver` since STORY-89 — "a stored row stopped being pending" — and the
 * wallet runs the *same* reconciliation over the same payment and had no equivalent. So for one
 * transaction, the merchant's app was told and the customer's app was not.
 *
 * It matters more since STORY-113/§19: unresolved wallet rows are now **visible** in history, so a
 * customer can be looking at a row that says "unresolved" at the moment the sweep resolves it.
 *
 * The design choices are the merchant observer's, unchanged — see
 * [co.veyra.softpos.payment.sdk.merchant.TransactionResolvedObserver] for the full reasoning:
 * registered once at SDK level (a payment can resolve days later, in a process that did not take
 * it), carries its identifier (the sweep walks every open row), no replay on registration (a dead
 * app is served by reading history at startup — §16), and one fire site.
 *
 * That fire site is [WalletHistoryStore.updateStatus], the single point where a wallet row's
 * outcome is written — the Android `TransactionStatusPollService`, the shared
 * `WalletPendingOutcomeSweeper`, STORY-113's on-demand `refreshTransactionStatus` and the MPM push
 * all go through it — so one hook covers every rail on both platforms (§14).
 *
 * **Only a genuine pending → final transition fires**, decided by the *shared*
 * `PaymentPollPolicy.isOpen` rather than a second reading of what "open" means (§19: "express
 * 'still open' once"). A `PENDING` → `PENDING` poll-count bump fires nothing, and neither does a
 * re-write of an already-final row.
 *
 * Additive and optional (§6). **Delivered on the main thread on every surface**, hopped in Kotlin
 * via the shared [co.veyra.common.observer.ObserverDelivery] hop on Android and the iOS facade's
 * `Dispatchers.Main` scope on iOS (ISSUE-182).
 */
